const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What kinds of concrete slabs do you pour?',
    a: 'Garage slabs, shed and workshop pads, hot tub and equipment pads, addition floors, and parking pads. If it needs to be flat, level and durable, we pour it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How thick should a concrete slab be?',
    a: 'It depends on the load. A shed pad needs less than a garage slab that carries vehicles. We spec the thickness and reinforcement for what the slab will actually hold, and we tell you why.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Will my slab crack?',
    a: 'Concrete shrinks as it cures, so we place control joints to guide any cracking into planned, straight lines. With a compacted base and the right mix, a slab should stay sound for decades.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can you pour a slab for a prefab garage or shed?',
    a: 'Yes, that is one of our most common slab jobs. We pour to the exact footprint the manufacturer specifies, level and square, so the building goes up without a fight.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long before I can build or park on a new slab?',
    a: 'Light foot traffic after 24 to 48 hours. Wait about a week before framing or parking a vehicle, and up to a month for full design strength. We leave clear guidance when we finish.',
  },
];
